en vaccinated  that usually just  pops up for a short time and then  disappears whereas we're seeing  genetically related viruses persistently  over a period of months now  and that strongly suggests that there's  transmission going on between people  within the uk and in this area of london  in particular and how risky is that  because there is a widespread  vaccination program against polio in  this country isn't there  yes the risks are low because just a  very large majority of people have been  while you know have been vaccinated  adequately to protect them but  nevertheless there are people who  haven't been  vaccinated at all and others that have  not been fully vaccinated  and these kind of viruses  can actually cause polio even though  they come from the vaccine they can  gradually  evolve back towards virulence and cause  paralysis so this is something we do  need to pay attention to and in the  short term  people should check that their children  have had all their schedule vaccinations  particularly if they're in north london  but elsewhere as well just to minimize  any possible risk  and remind us what we're talking about  here with polio because we've not had  many cases of it for us for some time  remind us what kind of a disease this is  yeah i mean vaccines are a victim of  their own success aren't they we we  constantly forget about the diseases  that used to exist that have disappeared  as a result of vaccinations and polio is  a good example of that it causes a an  illness with fever a bit like flu  and most people who get it make a full  recovery without any serious problems  but unfortunately a minority of people  can get this paralysis usually affects  the legs but it can affect other parts  of the body  and in some cases it's permanent so that  you get weakness of one or other of the  limbs  people can't move normally and then even  though they recover from the infection  that can on some cases be a permanent  problem so it's a serious illnes